Rage is a single-threaded, Fiber-based Ruby web framework running on Iodine. This is
framework code: it runs on every request of every app built on Rage.

Rationale for the rules below lives in [CONTRIBUTING.md]({{RAGE_ROOT}}CONTRIBUTING.md) and
[ARCHITECTURE.md]({{RAGE_ROOT}}ARCHITECTURE.md). Read them before your first larger
change. The rage-agent-kit skills cover file-specific conventions. They load when you edit the
files they cover; when you plan or review without editing, invoke the matching one with the
Skill tool.

## The flow

Larger work is carried through phases that the engineer invokes deliberately, each of
which stops for their approval before the next runs. Never start a phase because the previous
one finished, and never fold one into another task. The phases are the `/rage-agent-kit:`
commands in this session's command list; `/rage-agent-kit:feature:new` starts a new piece of
work.

The flow's documents live in `rage-feature-specs/`, a git checkout of the shared specs repo in
the **session's working directory**. Phases edit those files but never commit them. Do not read
a feature whose `spec.md` is `done` unless explicitly asked — completed work speaks through its
component document (`rage-feature-specs/components/`), its ADRs, and the code. If
`rage-feature-specs/` is here and you make a larger change outside the flow, read the component
document for that part of the framework first; the code is still the final source of truth.

Run `/rage-agent-kit:feature:status` when picking up work someone left half-finished.

## Stop and ask before

- Breaking any public API, method signature, config key, or wire format. Additive by
  default: new keyword arguments must be optional and default to current behavior. If the
  task cannot be solved additively, or additive would leave a worse API, say so and propose
  the break — what breaks, who is affected, the deprecation shim, and which release it
  targets. `Rage.load_middlewares` in [lib/rage-rb.rb]({{RAGE_ROOT}}lib/rage-rb.rb) is the
  pattern to follow. Proposing a break is a valid outcome; shipping one silently is not. This
  applies to surfaces in a released gem: one added since the latest release is not public yet
  and can still change without a shim — say so. When you cannot tell, treat it as released.
- Adding a gem to `{{RAGE_ROOT}}Gemfile` or `{{RAGE_ROOT}}rage.gemspec`. Rage's central
  promise is one process with no Redis and no separate workers, so a feature that needs an
  external service to function by default breaks the product, not just the dependency list.
  Iodine (`rage-iodine`) is already a dependency and part of the runtime; use its API freely.
- Introducing an abstraction, adapter, registry, or config knob the task does not require.
  Duplication beats the wrong abstraction — justify it or skip it.
- Using anything newer than Ruby 3.3.0.

## Ruby 3.3.0 is the floor

Set by `required_ruby_version` in [rage.gemspec]({{RAGE_ROOT}}rage.gemspec) and
`TargetRubyVersion` in [.rubocop.yml]({{RAGE_ROOT}}.rubocop.yml); CI runs 3.3.0, 3.3, 3.4, and
4.0. Verify recent features exist in 3.3.0 — for example `it` as an implicit block parameter
is 3.4+.

## Blocking I/O

One blocking call freezes the worker and every request in it. Audit every new call site and
report the result — "none" is a valid result, silence is not. This applies everywhere on the
reactor, not just to files under `{{RAGE_ROOT}}lib/rage/middleware`.

The list below is the canonical one; other rage-agent-kit skills refer to it rather than
restating it. Flag as worker-freezing: native extension calls (DB drivers, crypto,
compression, FFI); `Thread.new`; `system`, backticks, `Process.spawn`/`wait`, `IO.popen`;
blocking `flock` (without `LOCK_NB`), `fsync`, large file reads and writes; long CPU-bound
loops; client libraries with their own thread pool or `IO.select`.

Ways to move a blocking call off the reactor, in order of preference: scheduler-aware I/O;
`Rage::Deferred`, when the caller does not need the result.

Two things look like ways to do this, but framework code cannot rely on either.
`blocking_operation_wait`
([lib/rage/fiber_scheduler.rb]({{RAGE_ROOT}}lib/rage/fiber_scheduler.rb)) sends native calls
that can run outside the GVL to `Iodine::WorkerPool`, and the calling fiber yields until the
pool finishes — but Ruby decides which calls go there, and it runs only when an app turns on
`config.blocking_operation_pool` on a Ruby version that supports it. `Fiber.new(blocking: true)`
runs without the scheduler, so a blocking call inside it still blocks the worker; Rage uses it
only where that is acceptable (the IRB console, the RSpec helpers, the development-only Rails
reload), and it never belongs on the request path in production.

Fiber waits deadlock silently — Ruby's deadlock detector does not cover fibers under a
scheduler. Before adding any wait, park, resource pool, pub/sub wake-up, or file lock, use this
plugin's `deadlocks` skill.

## Performance

- Do work at boot, not per request. See `RageController::API.__register_action`.
- A feature must cost nothing to users who do not enable it. Generate a method with or
  without the feature rather than branching on every request — see `Rage::Logger#rebuild!`
  and `Rage::Telemetry::Tracer#setup`.
- No avoidable allocations on the happy path.
- Boot time matters too. Moving work to boot makes requests cheaper and startup slower;
  when the two conflict, say which you chose and why.

## Conventions

- `# frozen_string_literal: true` in every new file. Nothing enforces this — the cop is off,
  so a green `rubocop` run proves nothing here. Check it by hand.
- Formatting is whatever `bundle exec rubocop` accepts (`DisabledByDefault`, explicit
  allowlist).
- Internal API is `__`-prefixed and tagged `# @private`; internal ivars are `@__`;
  Fiber-locals are namespaced `:__rage_*`.
- User-facing methods need YARD `@param`/`@return`/`@example` tags. Write the tags as part of
  the change; running `yardoc` to validate them is a final-stage step (see Commands).
- Active Record is not the only ORM — do not assume it is loaded.
- `{{RAGE_ROOT}}docs/` holds the framework's own design docs. It is unrelated to the flow's
  `rage-feature-specs/` described above — do not confuse the two, and never write flow
  documents into the checkout. When editing a framework design doc, read it once
  and patch in place: do not regenerate sections that did not change, do not number or
  renumber headings, and do not redraw a diagram unless the actors or control flow changed.

## Writing for people

Rage is an open source project with contributors around the world, and many of them do not
know English at an advanced level. Keep the vocabulary at about **B2 level** in everything a
person reads: [CONTRIBUTING.md]({{RAGE_ROOT}}CONTRIBUTING.md), [README.md]({{RAGE_ROOT}}README.md),
files under `{{RAGE_ROOT}}docs/`, `{{RAGE_ROOT}}CHANGELOG.md` entries, YARD text, commit
messages, pull request descriptions, and your own replies in this session.

- Use the common word when it says the same thing: "larger", not "non-trivial"; "in
  progress", not "in flight"; "project rules", not "house rules".
- Technical terms the reader needs (fiber, reactor, spec, ADR, checkout) are fine. Do not
  replace them.
- Longer sentences are fine. This is about vocabulary, not about cutting everything into
  short sentences.
- Simpler wording must keep the exact meaning. Never drop or change a detail to make a
  sentence easier.

## Commands

These are the verification tools this project has, not a checklist to run. **Do not run them
unprompted during feature implementation.** `yardoc --fail-on-warning` and any RSpec
invocation are final-stage verification steps — run them separately, and only when the user
explicitly asks for that verification in this session. Never claim coverage you did not
actually run. Writing tests is a separate step too: do not add or change tests as part of a
change unless asked — tests are written in `/rage-agent-kit:task:test-core` (the core
criteria, before the code) and `/rage-agent-kit:task:test-edge` (the rest, after the code).

Entering a flow phase that authorizes a command *is* that explicit request, for the scope
that phase states and no wider. Nothing authorizes the full suite. Outside a phase, the rule
above stands unchanged.

```bash
bundle exec rake                      # default task: RSpec only. CI runs rubocop and yardoc as separate jobs.
bundle exec rubocop
bundle exec yardoc --fail-on-warning
bundle exec rake appraise             # spec/ext against Active Record 7.1-8.1
```

A green local run is not full coverage: parts of the suite are excluded by default, and others
skip when external services are missing. This plugin's `specs` skill lists what actually runs —
read it before reporting a result, and never claim specs passed that did not actually run.

Never commit spec artifacts such as `{{RAGE_ROOT}}storage/deferred-*`.

## Agent process rules

- Keep all work visible via `git status`/`git diff` in the repo the user has open. If you use
  an isolated git worktree (e.g. via an `isolation: "worktree"` agent option), do not report a
  task as done while the result sits unmerged in that worktree. Either merge the changes back
  into the checkout the user is looking at, or explicitly tell them the worktree's path and
  branch name and how to inspect it (e.g. `git diff main <worktree-branch>`). A worktree's
  changes never show up in the user's editor or `git status` on their own.
- Default to repo- and cwd-scoped lookups (`git` commands, ripgrep within the repo, known
  config paths) instead of broad filesystem scans. Never run a blind `find /` (or similar)
  sweep as a first resort — reach for a targeted command first (e.g. `git worktree list` to
  find a worktree, not a filesystem search).
